[TITLE:e200z0 Manual Extracted Data - Critical Specifications]

[INTRO]
This document contains key specifications extracted directly from the e200z0 Core
Reference Manual (E200Z0CRM, Rev. 0) for validation against LLVM implementation.

MANUAL_SOURCE: https://www.elektronikjk.com/elementy_czynne/IC/E200Z0.pdf
EXTRACTION_DATE: 2025-11-02
EXTRACTION_METHOD: pdftotext + manual review

[CHAPTER-4-TIMING]
UID: CH4-TIMING

TITLE: Instruction Timing Specifications

LOCATION: Chapter 4.2 (Execution Units)

INTEGER_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Simple integer (adds, subtracts, compares, shifts, rotates, count leading zeros): 
  1 cycle latency, single cycle execution
  
- Multiply instructions: 
  Latency: 1-4 cycles (data-dependent, early out)
  Worst case: 4 cycles
  Throughput: Variable based on data
  
- Divide instructions:
  Latency: 5-34 cycles (data-dependent)
  Worst case: 34 cycles
  Throughput: Blocking (pipeline unavailable during divide)

LOAD_STORE_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Load instructions:
  Latency: 1 cycle (when free of EA calculation dependency)
  Throughput: 1 per cycle (maximum)
  Note: Load followed by dependent instruction does not incur pipeline stall,
        except when dependent instruction uses load data for EA calculation
        (then 1 cycle "register-busy" pipeline stall)
  
- Store instructions:
  Latency: Similar to load (1 cycle when free of EA dependency)
  Throughput: 1 per cycle (maximum)
  Store data can be fed-forward from immediately preceding load (no stall)

BRANCH_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Conditional branches: 1 or 2 cycles
- Simple change of flow instructions: 2 cycles to refill pipeline
- Branch target prefetching can reduce timing for taken branches

VALIDATION_STATUS:
- ✓ Integer simple: 1 cycle - MATCHES
- ✓ Multiply: 1-4 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 4 cycles worst case - OK, but should note early out)
- ✓ Divide: 5-34 cycles - MATCHES (code correctly uses worst case 34)
- ✗ Load: 1 cycle - DISCREPANCY (code says 3 cycles)
- ✗ Store: 1 cycle - DISCREPANCY (code says 3 cycles)
- ✓ Branch: 1-2 cycles - MATCHES

[CHAPTER-4-PIPELINE]
UID: CH4-PIPELINE

TITLE: Pipeline Stages

LOCATION: Chapter 4.3, Table 4-1

STAGES:
1. IFETCH - Instruction Fetch From Memory
2. DECODE/EA - Instruction Decode / Register Read/ Operand Forwarding / EA Calculation
3. EXECUTE/MEM - Instruction Execution / Memory Access
4. WB - Write Back to Registers

VALIDATION_STATUS: ✓ MATCHES
- Code has: IF, ID, EX, WB (4 stages)
- Manual confirms: IFETCH, DECODE/EA, EXECUTE/MEM, WB
- Note: Memory operations occur in EXECUTE/MEM stage (no separate MEM stage)

[CHAPTER-5-INTERRUPTS]
UID: CH5-INTERRUPTS

TITLE: Interrupt Handling

LOCATION: Chapter 5.7 (Interrupt Definitions)

CRITICAL_INTERRUPT (IVOR0):
- Uses CSRR0/CSRR1 (Critical Save/Restore Registers)
- Hardware automatically saves:
  - CSRR0: Instruction address
  - CSRR1: MSR contents
- Return instruction: RFCI (Return From Critical Interrupt)

EXTERNAL_INTERRUPT (IVOR4):
- Should use SRR0/SRR1 (Standard Save/Restore Registers)
- Hardware automatically saves:
  - SRR0: Instruction address (if external interrupt)
  - SRR1: MSR contents (if external interrupt)
- Return instruction: RFI (Return From Interrupt)

NOTE: e200z0 manual confirms same pattern as e200z4:
- Critical interrupts use CSRR0/CSRR1 → RFCI
- External interrupts use SRR0/SRR1 → RFI

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ NEEDS IMPLEMENTATION
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE: No differentiation between critical and external interrupts

[CRITICAL-DISCREPANCIES]
UID: DISCREPANCIES

DISCREPANCY_001: Load Instruction Latency
Manual: 1 cycle latency (when free of EA dependency)
Code: 3 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z0.td:149-154)
Impact: WCET analysis will be highly pessimistic (3x slower)
Priority: CRITICAL

DISCREPANCY_002: Store Instruction Latency
Manual: 1 cycle latency (similar to load)
Code: 3 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z0.td:171-175)
Impact: WCET analysis will be highly pessimistic (3x slower)
Priority: CRITICAL

DISCREPANCY_003: Multiply Instruction Latency
Manual: 1-4 cycles (data-dependent, early out, worst case 4)
Code: 4 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z0.td:84-91)
Status: Acceptable (matches worst case), but should note early out capability

DISCREPANCY_004: Interrupt Type Differentiation
Manual: Critical uses CSRR0/CSRR1+RFCI, External uses SRR0/SRR1+RFI
Code: All interrupts use RFI (no differentiation)
Impact: Incorrect interrupt handling for critical interrupts
Priority: HIGH

